The KATRIN experiment has recently established a stringent upper limit on the electron antineutrino mass, $m_{
u_e} < 0.8$ eV at 90% confidence level. This result is examined in the context of the minimal three-neutrino framework, where the mass hierarchy remains undetermined. We demonstrate that, under the assumption of a normal mass hierarchy, the KATRIN bound necessitates a lower limit on the lightest neutrino mass of $m_{
u_1} > 0.006$ eV. Conversely, if the hierarchy is inverted, the bound implies $m_{
u_3} > 0.001$ eV. Furthermore, we analyze the implications for the sum of neutrino masses, $\sum m_{
u}$, and the effective electron neutrino mass, $m_{ eta}$, within the three-neutrino paradigm.

